Routine Optimizations for increased performance under Applesoft BASIC

RING BUFFER FOR SNAKE COORDINATES:

A ring buffer is used to manage snake position through 2 arrays of coordinates. Each array contains 100 positions ranging from 0..99. Snake movement is accomplished by moving head and tail pointer one position to the right, so no coordinate copying is required. Once a pointer reaches 100, it circles back to zero. Snake growth is also quite simple under this approach, as it is achieved during Process Movement by updating only the head and not the tail. Both processes become O(1) and efficient for Applesoft speed.

MOVING WALLS:

When walls start moving at level 8, they use a reimplementation of the snake movement. Since they are fixed in length, no ring buffer is required, just head and tail pointers to their current position on screen. W1 and W2 arrays keep track of snake occupancy on wall gaps to stop wall movement.

COLLISION DETECTION

Collision detection is simplified for O(1) complexity by only checking head coordinates against previous existing colour on screen. If black, there is no collision. Red is collision with fruit which causes the eat processing adding score, spawning a new fruit and growing the snake. Any other collision is treated as cause for snake to die, leading to game over routine. This allows the use of multiple colors for walls and other obstacles.

SCREEN UPDATES

To maximize performance, screen updates only apply changes to previous frame. Most of the time, this is just snake movement (erase tail and plot new head position). However, on some levels other movement may occur for barriers and walls. Walls use snake movement routine for efficiency.

INPUT ROUTINE

Uses keyboard strobe for efficiency. When no key has been pressed after last strobe, routine is skipped. Once a key is detected, direction is processed and return abort further key testing.



